output corrections remain with #7/#181/#109/#159; this known boundary is not consumed or recreated.\n\n## L2 — One ordinary session with explicit absence-policy reflection\n\nThe course's adaptive question order and shuffled displayed option letters are observed, not forced. Bind each actual prompt uniquely to the sealed course identity; bind data-choice to original option identity and text. Use trusted visible option and Next controls. Do not inject answer state or call internal answer/export functions.\n\nUse this fixed answer scenario from the root's independent authority: choose original option 0 for null-member-11 (correct is 2); choose the correct original option for all other questions.\n\nnull-member-01: correct 2; choose 2; correct\nnull-member-02: correct 1; choose 1; correct\nnull-member-03: correct 0; choose 0; correct\nnull-member-04: correct 3; choose 3; correct\nnull-member-05: correct 1; choose 1; correct\nnull-member-06: correct 0; choose 0; correct\nnull-member-07: correct 2; choose 2; correct\nnull-member-08: correct 3; choose 3; correct\nnull-member-09: correct 0; choose 0; correct\nnull-member-10: correct 1; choose 1; correct\nnull-member-11: correct 2; choose 0; needs review\nnull-member-12: correct 3; choose 3; correct\n\nEvery item appears once. Require twelve review entries, eleven correct first answers and one Needs review entry for null-member-11. Compare every prompt, first choice, correct choice and explanation with the sealed content and recorded UI actions. Preserve the observed adaptive order. These counts describe this receiving session and are not a learning-efficacy or ability measurement.\n\nExpand the existing null-member-11 review and enter this exact fictional receiver-authored item reflection through its rendered field, preserving the newline:\nI treated a filtered NOT IN as a universal absence policy. On an empty blocked table, even a NULL request code passes NOT IN.\nI will state the missing-code policy first and keep the outer code IS NOT NULL guard when unknown requests must be excluded.\n\nEnter this exact fictional application writing through the original Apply it in your own words field:\nFor an eligibility report, I would keep requests with a known code and no equal blocked code. A correlated NOT EXISTS makes row absence explicit; the outer IS NOT NULL check states the policy for missing request codes.\n\nThe original imported-deck application prompt is:\nChoose one connection from this deck and explain how it relates to another idea in your own words.\n\nThese writings are test-session examples, not statements attributed to Jacob. Use rendered focused controls and Input.insertText or equivalent trusted browser text actuation, recording actual input events. Any synthetic control actuation must be disclosed exactly. Writing remains separate from the scored first answers. No retry/practice session, restore, old edit-distance case or learner mechanics matrix is run.\n\n## L3 — One complete physical study-notes handoff\n\nActivate the original visible Download study notes (.txt) button once. Require an actual completed browser download into a fresh owned directory, with event, path, bytes, SHA256 and Git identity. A success status or in-memory Blob alone is insufficient.\n\nThe filename is recallweave-study-notes-YYYY-MM-DD.txt with the original export's actual UTC date. Observe the Saved timestamp in the physical file and require it to lie between native/browser times bounding this click and completed download. Do not fix the clock, invent a timestamp or rerender a second file for comparison.\n\nCompare the entire physical UTF-8 file, including its final LF, against an independently assembled expected layout derived by reading the unchanged original createStudyNotes source, the sealed lesson and actual observed review/percentage values. Do not call the original exporter to generate the expected text. The physical export does not print item IDs; bind each exported prompt/record to its unique observed review identity.\n\nThe exact original layout includes:\n- \"RecallWeave — study notes\", the exact course title and actual ISO Saved time.\n- FIRST SESSION: \"11 of 12 connections correct on the first try.\" and the original session/ability limitation.\n- The original ESTIMATED MASTERY header, both model-state cautions and all four concept labels in source order with observed original displayed percentages. No numerical model replay is required.\n- PRACTICE: \"Not started. 1 missed connection is available for practice.\" and the original separate-practice explanation.\n- All twelve records in actual review order, with numbered prompt, concept, literal first/correct answer text, correct/needs-review status, full explanation and transfer.\n- The original reflection label for each record. null-member-11 contains the two exact lines above, each prefixed by the original \"  > \"; all other item writing is \"  Not written.\". The missed record alone includes \"Practice answer: not recorded.\".\n- YOUR APPLICATION REFLECTION — NOT SCORED, the exact original prompt and literal application writing with the original prefix.\n- DECK ATTRIBUTION with the course's exact attribution and license.\n- The exact original final sentence: \"Saved from this browser session. The download does not upload the session or restore it after a refresh.\"\n\nRetain the actual whole file, independently expected whole text, meaningful original screenshots and actual events. This is a readable saved record; no refresh-resume, backup restore, automatic persistence or combined-pack claim is made.\n\n## L4 — Exact preservation and owned closure\n\nReobserve recorded first answers after writing/download where needed; they must be unchanged. Close only this private browser and its observed owned descendants, recording actual child exits and independent absence. The entry child must be reaped/absent. The route has no application or debugging listener: use the already qualified private inherited CDP pipes.\n\nCompare all nineteen installed files and all four delivered files byte-for-byte, with native modes and exact mtime strings, before and after. Preserve selected runtime identity and new receiver source/authority bytes. No original path is an output destination and no existing profile is used.\n\nRecord exceptions, console entries, resource failures, application requests and browser OS stderr separately. Do not claim global zero stderr, zero background traffic, or successful unexecuted groups. Optional external links are not activated. Preserve any observer/product failure and all completed groups; a later narrow continuation requires its own explicit reviewed freeze and does not erase this invocation.\n\n## Capacity, serialization and custody\n\nAuthor delivery: at most 256 KiB in the exclusive delivery root; only three maintained files plus DELIVERY.json. One original-parser child, Node 26.3.0 using the admitted existing /opt/homebrew/bin/node, runs from /tmp with an eight-second child timeout; the complete staging interval is bounded at twenty seconds. Python 3.13.7 runs isolated with -I -B. No dependency download or real database is involved.\n\nReceiver-exclusive proposed root:\n /Users/me/Developer/recallweave-sql-null-membership-receiving-c945953fdeb7\n\nBefore any source materialization and browser launch require at least 256 MiB free disk and at least 2 GiB conservative free+inactive+speculative RAM. Retain the actual native guard values and unchanged floors. Receiver source/evidence cap 2 MiB; separate private Chrome profile/cache/download cap 96 MiB; one ordinary session no longer than 180 seconds. Use already qualified Chrome/154.0.8037.99 through private pipes with a fresh full executable pin. Do not lower guards, clean peers, modify a donor, start a shared service or install dependencies.\n\nRoot controls the interval and source publication. Control currently owns the CaptureSuite continuation interval, so this proposal authorizes